Why not use one listener?
Imagine one event needs to be delivered to 1,000 customer endpoints.
- With separate listeners: A can fan out the event into 1,000 delivery tasks. B processes those tasks concurrently, subject to endpoint rate limits and concurrency caps.
- With one combined listener: event processing and outbound HTTP calls would be coupled. Slow or unresponsive customer endpoints could occupy consumers and delay processing of new incoming events.

Service 1 (Event Service)
        |
        v
      RabbitMQ
        |
        | events.in
        v
Service 2 (Delivery Service)
        |
        +-- Listener A: Fan-out
        |      |
        |      +--> Store event and create delivery records
        |      |
        |      +--> Publish DeliverWebhook messages
        |                    |
        |                    v
        |                 RabbitMQ
        |               delivery.main
        |                    |
        |                    v
        +-- Listener B: Delivery workers
                       |
                       +--> Apply per-endpoint controls
                       +--> Send HTTP webhook
                       +--> Record result / schedule retry